// csharp-rt.metajs -- C#'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the fifth language after the Lua pilot,
// PHP, Swift and Java.
//
// languages/csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 68 js_* externs for
// tests/csharp-test-full.cs. 35 of them are generic primitives
// languages/lib/runtime.c already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 33
// are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/csharp-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-csharp-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/csharp-rt.ll that csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol, so
// nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are
// deliberately named cs*/cp*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrtcsharp.go, plus abnf/jsrtjvm.go for
// the boxed double and the js_csadd / js_cslen / js_char / js_char_code /
// js_jchareq / js_jadd / js_mcall / js_supercall / js_is_type / js_arr_new_n /
// js_dict_new / js_pyget / js_pyset / js_pylen / js_has / js_csslice /
// js_gospread block of abnf/jsrt.go. This file matches them function for
// function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in full.
//
// ----- the value model (abnf/jsrtcsharp.go's own header) ---------------------
//
//     null / undefined     ==  a null reference
//     a PLAIN NUMBER       ==  an `int`, i.e. a SIGNED 32 BIT value. This is C#'s
//                              invariant and it is deliberately NOT Go's, where a
//                              plain number is a 64 bit int.
//     a tag 13 box         ==  EVERY other integral type: long, ulong, uint,
//                              short, ushort, byte (UNSIGNED in C#) and sbyte.
//     a tag 14 box         ==  double / float / decimal, STYLE 2 (C#).
//     a {__char} box       ==  char. The Go twin uses jsChar, a Go TYPE. Layer 2
//                              cannot make a primitive type, so this is the same
//                              OBJECT box languages/csharp-interpreter.abnf and
//                              languages/lib/java-rt.metajs already use.
//     a {__dict} object    ==  Dictionary<K,V>: keys/vals parallel arrays.
//     an array             ==  int[] / List<T> alike (this runtime has one).
//     an {__isclass} obj   ==  a type descriptor: __name, __super, __isrecord.
//     any other object     ==  an instance; its descriptor is __class.
//
// ----- THE LONG PROBLEM, and how `sintRaw` removed it ------------------------
//
// C#'s `long` must stay DISTINGUISHABLE from `int` at every value, because
// `1000000L * 1000000L` is 1000000000000 while `1000000 * 1000000` wraps to
// -727379968. The floor's si_norm - the ONE place the sized-integer invariant is
// applied - UNBOXES a signed 64 bit value a double holds exactly, so
// `sint(0, 5, 64, 0)` is the plain number 5, indistinguishable from `int` 5.
//
// languages/lib/java-rt.metajs met that wall first and worked around it by
// marking a long box UNSIGNED - the one shape si_norm will not unbox - and then
// re-supplying six SIGNED operations (/ % >>, compare, decimal text, double
// reading) in about 90 lines. C# does NOT do that, because the floor now has
//
//     sintRaw(hi, lo, bits, unsigned)   si_trunc, then si_make: a tag 13 cell
//                                       ALWAYS, never si_norm's unboxing arm
//
// so a C# long is an honest {w: 64, u: 0} box. Every one of the six operations is
// then the floor's own: sintOp's `/` and `%` and `>>` already branch on the cell's
// signedness, sintCmp already compares signed when the type is, sintStr already
// prints the minus sign and sintNum already reads the negative half. csharp-rt has
// no jl* layer at all. THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT the floor request asked for:
// ~90 lines saved, and - the part that matters more - the JAVA SECOND-ORDER HAZARD
// IS GONE WITH IT. A floor extern that reads a tag 13 cell numerically (js_csflo
// is C#'s one) sees a MAGNITUDE when the cell is the marked-unsigned shape; with
// sintRaw the cell is signed, so to_number reads it correctly and no js_*num
// wrapper is owed. go / kotlin / dart should all take this route.
//
// ----- generators: the floor drives them, and layer 2 owes NOTHING -----------
//
// C# is the first language to reach the coroutine primitive that landed in the
// floor (tags 15/16). It costs this file zero lines, and the reason is worth
// copying for js / ts / python, which follow: csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf lowers the
// WHOLE iterator protocol in the EMITTER, over the floor's own member syntax -
//
//     js_genfn(js_closure(...))                 the iterator method
//     js_typeof(js_get(g, "next")) === "function"   "is this a generator?"
//     js_call(js_get(g, "next"), [])            MoveNext()
//     js_get(step, "value") / js_get(step, "done")
//
// The floor's get_member answers `next` on a tag 15 cell with a bound method, so
// every one of those is already a floor extern. `yield break` is an ordinary
// return and `.Current` is a field the emitter parks on its own cursor object.
// A THROW out of a generator body needs no case either: gen_resume re-raises it on
// the RESUMER's stack, so it comes out of the `next()` call site like any other.
//
// ----- what C# did NOT need, checked rather than assumed ---------------------
//
//  1. NO `__raw` truthy shim. `grep -n truthyExt languages/csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf`
//     is empty, so core.truthyExt keeps compile-core's default "js_truthy", which
//     is the C FLOOR'S OWN function and already answers the raw 1/0 the emitted
//     `icmp ne ..., 0` compares. Swift and Java were the same; Lua and PHP are the
//     two that override it.
//  2. ONE `_raw` PARAMETER, js_char's, exactly like Java. csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf
//     writes the code point with handle(k), and 0..3 are the handles of
//     undefined/null/false/true - so without the suffix the callee would see one of
//     those. Every other handle(k) in that grammar goes to js_closure or js_arg,
//     which are floor externs.
//  3. NO scope-probe extern to lower. C#'s emitter already probes the scope
//     in IR (js_scope_typeof against "undefined" at csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf:4109);
//     PHP's and Swift's lowering is already done here, so there was nothing left.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the TYPE
// CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one branch and
// an object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI, so every
// `return` carries its value on the same line and a value-less early return is
// BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its
// from-index, array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). It supplies
// the externs every language implements identically, parameterised by the rtk*
// hooks below. Same mechanism ruby uses for `import "./regex.js"`.
import "./runtime.metajs"

// The bodies csharp and java share and NOBODY ELSE DOES, in their own module so
// the other eight importers of runtime.metajs pay nothing for them. The measured
// slope of the live-body tax, and the difference list that made the merge two
// knobs, are both at the head of that file.
import "./runtime-jvm.metajs"

// ----- runtime-jvm.metajs's two knobs ----------------------------------------
function rtjkCmp(op, l, r) { return csCmp(op, l, r) }
function rtjkFloOperand(v) { return csFloOperand(v) }

// ----- the rtk* hooks the shared runtime calls -------------------------------
function rtkStr(v) { return csJavaStr(v) }
function rtkNum(v) { return rtjNum(v) }
function rtkIsChar(v) { return csIsChar(v) }
function rtkIsArr(v) { return Array.isArray(v) }
// js_supercall's one knob. Only the four languages whose emitter actually emits
// js_supercall define it; see the note there.
function rtkCall(f, self, argv) { return f.apply(undefined, argv) }
// js_pyset's two knobs.
function rtkIsDict(v) { return csIsDict(v) }
function rtkDictFind(keys, k) { return csDictFind(keys, k) }

// ===================================================== the char box =========
//
// rtCharBox / rtIsCharBox are lib/runtime.metajs's; csChar and csIsChar were
// byte-identical to java's jvChar / jvIsChar and near-identical to kotlin's.

function csChar(code) { return rtCharBox(code) }

function csIsChar(v) { return rtIsCharBox(v) }

// ===================================================== value readings =======

// rtjIsIntegral (jsrtcsharp.go): an operand of INTEGER arithmetic. A char is one,
// C#'s char being an integral type; a double is not. SHARED with java -
// lib/runtime-jvm.metajs; the two copies had ZERO differences.

// csRaw hands the sint* builtins something they can read. si_val knows tag 3, tag
// 13 and tag 14 - it does NOT know the {__char} object box, which would read as
// NaN, so the code point is taken off it here and nowhere else.
function csRaw(v) {
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    return v
}

// csSzW / csSzU: the declared width and signedness of an operand. A plain number
// and a char both answer "signed 32", which is what jsrtcsharp.go's csSzW/csSzU do
// by falling through their jsGInt case.
function csSzW(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintWidth(v) }
    return 32
}

function csSzU(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintUns(v) }
    return false
}

// csNorm applies the C# invariant: a signed 32 bit result is a PLAIN NUMBER,
// everything else is a box. sintConv would unbox a signed 64 bit value that a
// double holds exactly (si_norm), so the result is re-boxed with sintRaw whenever
// the invariant says it must stay one - which is the whole of what java-rt.metajs
// needed ninety lines to avoid.
function csNorm(v, w, u) {
    var t = sintConv(csRaw(v), w, u)
    if (w == 32) { if (!u) { return sintNum(t) } }
    if (sintIs(t)) { return t }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), w, u)
}

// csBox is jsrtcsharp.go's csBox: read a value at a given width and signedness,
// ALWAYS as a box (never a plain number, whatever the invariant would say).
function csBox(v, w, u) {
    var t = sintConv(csRaw(v), w, u)
    if (sintIs(t)) { return t }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), w, u)
}

// The double reading of anything numeric is rtjNum - SHARED with java,
// lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. The two copies differed in the char test and the string
// coercion and BOTH differences were identity: csIsChar and jvIsChar are each
// `rtIsCharBox(v)`, and java's jpStrNum was a one-line wrapper on parseFloat.

// ===================================================== BCL exceptions =======

// csThrow (jsrtcsharp.go): the BCL exception types are plain objects carrying
// __excname and Message in BOTH halves - the shape `new DivideByZeroException(msg)`
// builds (csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf:3138) and a catch clause reads.
function csThrow(name, msg) {
    var o = {}
    o["__excname"] = name
    o["Message"] = msg
    throw o
}

// ===================================================== the operators ========

// csPromote is ECMA-334 12.4.7.3 binary numeric promotion for two integral
// operands. The surprising rule is the uint one: `uint + int` is a LONG, because
// int does not fit in uint and uint does not fit in int. It is the only place C#
// differs from Java's much simpler "long if either is long, else int".
function csPromote(l, r) {
    var lw = csSzW(l)
    var lu = csSzU(l)
    var rw = csSzW(r)
    var ru = csSzU(r)
    if (lw == 64 && lu) { return {w: 64, u: true} }
    if (rw == 64 && ru) { return {w: 64, u: true} }
    if (lw == 64 || rw == 64) { return {w: 64, u: false} }
    if ((lw == 32 && lu) || (rw == 32 && ru)) {
        var other = l
        if (lw == 32 && lu) { other = r }
        // A char, a byte and a ushort convert to uint and stay there; a plain
        // number is an `int` and forces the pair up to long.
        if (csIsChar(other)) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
        if (sintIs(other)) {
            if (sintUns(other)) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
            if (sintWidth(other) > 32) { return {w: 32, u: true} }
            return {w: 64, u: false}
        }
        return {w: 64, u: false}
    }
    return {w: 32, u: false}
}

// The sintOp index of one arithmetic or bitwise operator.
function csOpCode(op) {
    if (op == "+") { return 0 }
    if (op == "-") { return 1 }
    if (op == "*") { return 2 }
    if (op == "/") { return 3 }
    if (op == "%") { return 4 }
    if (op == "&") { return 5 }
    if (op == "|") { return 6 }
    if (op == "^") { return 7 }
    return 0 - 1
}

// csArith: one binary arithmetic or bitwise operator on two INTEGRAL operands, at
// the promoted type and wrapping to it. ARITHMETIC IS UNCHECKED, C#'s default
// (ECMA-334 12.8.20): every operator wraps. Division by zero DOES throw (12.9.3).
function csArith(op, l, r) {
    var p = csPromote(l, r)
    var w = p.w
    var un = p.u
    var code = csOpCode(op)
    if (code < 0) {
        fail("js_csarith: unknown operator '" + op + "'")
        return undefined
    }
    if (w != 32 || un) {
        var a = csBox(l, w, un)
        var b = csBox(r, w, un)
        if (op == "/" || op == "%") {
            if (sintCmp(b, csBox(0, w, un)) == 0) {
                csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.")
            }
        }
        return csNorm(sintOp(code, a, b), w, un)
    }
    var x = csRaw(l)
    var y = csRaw(r)
    var xi = csNorm(x, 32, false)
    var yi = csNorm(y, 32, false)
    if (op == "+") { return rtWrap32(xi + yi) }
    if (op == "-") { return rtWrap32(xi - yi) }
    if (op == "*") { return Math.imul(xi, yi) }
    if (op == "/") {
        if (yi == 0) { csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.") }
        // int.MinValue / -1 overflows. ECMA-334 12.9.3 leaves the UNCHECKED outcome
        // implementation-defined between a System.OverflowException and "the
        // resulting value being that of the left operand"; the second is what BOTH
        // halves answer, and it is what Java specifies outright.
        if (xi == 0 - 2147483648) { if (yi == 0 - 1) { return xi } }
        return rtWrap32(Math.trunc(xi / yi))
    }
    if (op == "%") {
        if (yi == 0) { csThrow("DivideByZeroException", "Attempted to divide by zero.") }
        if (xi == 0 - 2147483648) { if (yi == 0 - 1) { return 0 } }
        return xi - Math.trunc(xi / yi) * yi
    }
    if (op == "&") { return xi & yi }
    if (op == "|") { return xi | yi }
    return xi ^ yi
}

// csShift is << >> >>>. The result type is the LEFT operand's alone after unary
// promotion (ECMA-334 12.11: sbyte/byte/short/ushort/char all become int), and the
// COUNT is MASKED - & 31 for an int/uint, & 63 for a long/ulong, so `1 << 32` is 1.
// `>>` on an UNSIGNED type is the LOGICAL shift, which is how C# spells what Java
// writes as `>>>`; C# 11's own `>>>` is that same operation on a signed type.
//
// Every arm goes through sintOp on a BOX rather than through MetaJS's own 32 bit
// operators, so the signed/unsigned choice is made once, by the cell's own flag.
function csShift(op, l, r) {
    var w = 32
    if (csSzW(l) == 64) { w = 64 }
    var un = false
    if (csSzW(l) >= 32) { un = csSzU(l) }
    var s = csShiftCount(r, w)
    if (op == ">>>") {
        // The logical shift whatever the declared signedness is: read the same bits
        // through an UNSIGNED cell of the same width.
        return csNorm(sintOp(10, csBox(l, w, true), s), w, un)
    }
    var a = csBox(l, w, un)
    if (op == "<<") { return csNorm(sintOp(9, a, s), w, un) }
    return csNorm(sintOp(10, a, s), w, un)
}

// giVal(r) & 31 / & 63. The count is read at 32 bits first, which is what every
// count in a C# program is, and then masked.
function csShiftCount(r, w) {
    var n = csNorm(r, 32, false)
    if (w == 64) { return n & 63 }
    return n & 31
}

// csCmp is the ordered comparison at the PROMOTED type. sintCmp on its own is not
// usable: it compares at the widest operand's width, so `byte b = 5; b < 1000`
// would truncate 1000 to a byte and answer false.
function csCmp(op, l, r) {
    var p = csPromote(l, r)
    var c = sintCmp(csBox(l, p.w, p.u), csBox(r, p.w, p.u))
    if (op == "<") { return c < 0 }
    if (op == ">") { return c > 0 }
    if (op == "<=") { return c <= 0 }
    if (op == ">=") { return c >= 0 }
    if (op == "==") { return c == 0 }
    return c != 0
}

// csConvTo is an explicit conversion to an integral type. An INTEGRAL source is a
// pure bit truncation (unchecked); a FLOATING POINT one whose value does not fit is
// "undefined behaviour" in ECMA-334 12.3.2, so this SATURATES - deterministic, and
// identical in both halves.
function csConvTo(v, w, u) {
    if (floIs(v)) {
        var n = floNum(v)
        if (n !== n) { n = 0 }
        if (n >= 18446744073709551616) { return csNorm(csAllOnes(), w, u) }
        if (n >= 9223372036854775808) {
            if (u) { return csNorm(csFromUDouble(n), w, u) }
            return csNorm(csMaxI64(), w, u)
        }
        if (n < 0 - 9223372036854775808) { return csNorm(csMinI64(), w, u) }
        return csNorm(csFromDouble(Math.trunc(n)), w, u)
    }
    return csNorm(v, w, u)
}

// ulong.MaxValue's bit pattern, i.e. Go's `p = -1`.
function csAllOnes() { return sintRaw(4294967295, 4294967295, 64, true) }
function csMaxI64() { return sintRaw(2147483647, 4294967295, 64, false) }
function csMinI64() { return sintRaw(2147483648, 0, 64, false) }

// An integral double in [-2^63, 2^63) as a 64 bit cell. Above 2^53 the double is
// already inexact and this reproduces that inexactness rather than inventing
// digits, which is what int64(math.Trunc(n)) does in the Go twin.
function csFromDouble(v) {
    var neg = false
    var a = v
    if (a < 0) {
        neg = true
        a = 0 - a
    }
    var hi = Math.floor(a / 4294967296)
    var lo = a - hi * 4294967296
    var x = sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, false)
    if (neg) { x = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false), x) }
    return x
}

// uint64(n) for the [2^63, 2^64) half, where the signed reading would overflow.
function csFromUDouble(v) {
    var hi = Math.floor(v / 4294967296)
    var lo = v - hi * 4294967296
    return sintRaw(hi, lo, 64, true)
}

// ===================================================== the boxed double =====

// jvmArith (abnf/jsrtjvm.go), reached whenever a double is on either side, is
// rtjArith - SHARED with java, lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. Its ONE knob is the
// operand mapper below, reached through rtjkFloOperand.

// floOp reads its operands with the floor's to_number, which knows tag 13 and tag
// 14 but NOT the {__char} object box. A tag 13 cell is read correctly - a C# long
// is an honest signed cell, unlike java-rt.metajs's marked-unsigned one - so only
// the char needs converting here.
function csFloOperand(v) {
    if (floIs(v)) { return v }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    return v
}

// ===================================================== the float WIDTH ======
//
// A C# `float` is System.Single, "an IEEE-754 binary32 value" (ECMA-334 8.3.7),
// and not a print style: 1.0f/3.0f is 0.33333334 where the double is
// 0.3333333333333333, and every arithmetic result rounds back to 24 significant
// bits. The VALUE still travels as a double (every float is exactly one), so the
// only thing that goes on the box is the WIDTH - and the tag 14 box already has a
// byte for a per-language print tag. STYLE 5 IS THE C# float. abnf/jsrtjvm.go's
// header is the specification; languages/lib/runtime.c is deliberately unchanged,
// which is why every rendering and every arithmetic result is intercepted HERE
// before the floor sees it: runtime.c's jf_text has no arm for style 5 and would
// print a float with the DOUBLE renderer. (That the floor is the wrong home is
// MEASURED, not preferred: c-to-llvm-ir.abnf computes every float at double
// precision, so the floor cannot spell (float)x at all - see docs/todo.md 1.1.)
//
// Three engines implement this one specification: the {__flo,__f32} box of
// languages/csharp-interpreter.abnf, jsJFlo{sty: floCSF} in abnf/jsrtjvm.go and
// abnf/jsrtcsharp.go, and this file. Nothing else builds a style 5 box, so java,
// kotlin and go are untouched by every line below.
//
// CONFIDENCE, stated because there is NO C# TOOLCHAIN ON THIS MACHINE. The
// ARITHMETIC and the ROUNDING are settled: a binary32 operation is the same
// operation in C# and in Java, and java 24.0.2 was the oracle for every value.
// The RENDERING is spec-cited only - ECMA-334 8.3.7 fixes the type but leaves
// ToString()'s text to the library - and csFloStr32 in abnf/jsrtjvm.go carries
// the full argument for each digit-placement decision.

// x * 2^k, EXACTLY - every factor is a power of two, so not one bit is lost. The
// chunking by 2^30 keeps the loop short for the ~280 halvings a subnormal float
// needs; a plain doubling loop would be just as exact and much slower.
function csScale2(x, k) {
    var v = x
    var n = k
    while (n >= 30) { v = v * 1073741824; n = n - 30 }
    while (n <= 0 - 30) { v = v / 1073741824; n = n + 30 }
    while (n > 0) { v = v * 2; n = n - 1 }
    while (n < 0) { v = v / 2; n = n + 1 }
    return v
}

// csF32Round is Math.fround: the double nearest x that a binary32 can hold,
// round-half-to-EVEN, with the subnormals and both overflows. It is what the Go
// twin spells `float64(float32(x))`.
//
// It is written on `*` and `/` by powers of two and a floor, because MetaJS has
// no access to a double's BITS - chapter 7.7 of docs/working-on-this-project.md,
// the same argument that keeps d_pow and d_frexp in the C floor. The significand
// is scaled until its unit in the last place is 1, rounded there, and scaled
// back; both scalings are exact, so the single rounding is the only one.
function csF32Round(x) {
    if (x !== x) { return x }
    if (x === 0) { return x }
    var zero = 0
    var inf = 1 / zero
    var neg = x < 0
    var a = neg ? 0 - x : x
    if (a === inf) { return x }
    var maxf = csScale2(16777215, 104)
    // 2^128 is above every rounding boundary and 2^-150 is the half-way point
    // below the smallest subnormal (which ties to even, i.e. to zero); both early
    // exits also bound the exponent loop below to ~280 turns.
    if (a >= csScale2(1, 128)) { return neg ? 0 - inf : inf }
    if (a <= csScale2(1, 0 - 150)) { return neg ? csNegZeroF() : 0 }
    var e = 0
    var t = a
    while (t >= 2) { t = t / 2; e = e + 1 }
    while (t < 1) { t = t * 2; e = e - 1 }
    var q = e - 23
    if (q < 0 - 149) { q = 0 - 149 }
    var s = csScale2(a, 0 - q)
    var f = Math.floor(s)
    var d = s - f
    if (d > 0.5) { f = f + 1 }
    if (d === 0.5) { if (f - Math.floor(f / 2) * 2 !== 0) { f = f + 1 } }
    var r = csScale2(f, q)
    if (r > maxf) { return neg ? 0 - inf : inf }
    if (r === 0) { return neg ? csNegZeroF() : 0 }
    return neg ? 0 - r : r
}

// -0.0. `0 - 0.0` is +0.0 and `x * 0` keeps an integral zero, so the working
// spelling is a division - docs/working-on-this-project.md chapter 7.10.
function csNegZeroF() { return 0 / (0 - 1) }

// The result style of a binary operation on two float-ish operands. ECMA-334
// 12.4.7.3 promotes to the WIDER type - "otherwise, if either operand is of type
// double, the other operand is converted to type double" comes BEFORE the float
// clause - so `float op double` is a double; the floor's jf_style_of takes the
// LEFT operand's style, which gets that pair backwards.
function csFloStyle2(l, r) {
    if (floIs(l)) {
        if (floStyle(l) == 5) {
            if (floIs(r)) { return floStyle(r) }
            return 5
        }
        return floStyle(l)
    }
    if (floIs(r)) { return floStyle(r) }
    return 2
}

// The fix-up applied to the floor's own floOp answer. It runs ONLY when that
// answer already claims style 5 - the one host call the double path pays - and
// style 5 can only come from a C# float, because jf_style_of took it off an
// operand. Two things are wrong with the floor's answer then, and both are
// ECMA-334 12.4.7.3:
//
//   float op DOUBLE is a double. jf_style_of takes the LEFT operand's style and
//   gets that pair backwards; the VALUE is already right, since a float widens to
//   a double exactly, so only the box is rebuilt.
//
//   float op INT converts the int to a FLOAT FIRST. Rounding only the result is a
//   different answer: `0.1f + 16777217` is 1.6777216E7, and 1.6777218E7 if the
//   int keeps its exact value into the addition. So the operation is redone on
//   two converted operands. (Settled with java 24.0.2, whose JLS 5.6.2 rule is
//   the same one.)
function csFloFix(op, rr, l, r) {
    if (floIs(r)) { if (floStyle(r) != 5) { return flo(floNum(rr), floStyle(r)) } }
    var a = flo(csF32Round(rtjNum(l)), 5)
    var b = flo(csF32Round(rtjNum(r)), 5)
    return flo(csF32Round(floNum(rtjArith(op, a, b))), 5)
}

// The same promotion for the RELATIONS and for `==`: a float operand converts the
// other side to a float, so `16777216f == 16777217` is TRUE. It answers false
// unless the promoted type really is float, so every double and every integral
// pair keeps the path it had.
function csFloPromotes(l, r) {
    if (!floIs(l) && !floIs(r)) { return false }
    if (csFloStyle2(l, r) != 5) { return false }
    if (!floIs(l) && !rtjIsIntegral(l)) { return false }
    if (!floIs(r) && !rtjIsIntegral(r)) { return false }
    return true
}

// ----- Single.ToString() ----------------------------------------------------
//
// The C# double renderer read at 24 significant bits: the SHORTEST decimal that
// reads back as the same float, plain inside the window and d.dddE+nn outside it,
// NO forced ".0" and no two-significant-digit minimum (both of those are Java's).
// abnf/jsrtjvm.go's csFloStr32 states what is settled and what is derived.
//
// THE DIGITS COME FROM `"" + a`, NOT FROM floPrec, and here that is SOUND -
// which is not the same reason this comment used to give. It used to say
// floPrec's two implementations DISAGREE (exactly n digits in
// abnf/commonscript.go against max(n, shortest) in languages/lib/runtime.c);
// `08e8fcb` unified them and both now answer exactly n, so either question could
// be asked here. What keeps `"" + a` is that LAYER 2's hosts are exact: natively
// it is the C floor's number-to-string and under llvm.Run it is the Go twin's,
// both strconv-grade shortest round-tripping forms. The INTERPRETER half is the
// one that could not use it, because its host is goja - see the csDecDigits
// comment in languages/csharp-interpreter.abnf and docs/todo.md 1.8.

// The shortest round-tripping decimal of the double a > 0, as DIGITS "e" EXP with
// EXP the decimal exponent of the FIRST digit.
function csDecDigits(a) {
    var s = "" + a
    var mant = s
    var exp = 0
    var ei = 0 - 1
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(i)
        if (c == "e") { ei = i }
        if (c == "E") { ei = i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (ei >= 0) {
        mant = s.substring(0, ei)
        var et = s.substring(ei + 1)
        if (et.charAt(0) == "+") { et = et.substring(1) }
        exp = parseInt(et, 10)
    }
    var digits = ""
    var pt = 0 - 1
    var j = 0
    while (j < mant.length) {
        var ch = mant.charAt(j)
        if (ch == ".") { pt = digits.length } else { if (ch != "-") { digits = digits + ch } }
        j = j + 1
    }
    if (pt < 0) { pt = digits.length }
    var lead = 0
    while (lead < digits.length - 1 && digits.charAt(lead) == "0") { lead = lead + 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(lead)
    pt = pt - lead
    var endz = digits.length
    while (endz > 1 && digits.charAt(endz - 1) == "0") { endz = endz - 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(0, endz)
    return digits + "e" + (pt + exp - 1)
}

// A DIGITS "e" EXP form rounded to n significant digits, half to even. The source
// is already the shortest form of the value, so a genuine tie can only be a
// decimal the double holds exactly.
function csRoundDig(s, n) {
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    var ds = s.substring(0, ei)
    var ex = parseInt(s.substring(ei + 1), 10)
    if (ds.length <= n) { return s }
    var keep = ds.substring(0, n)
    var nxt = ds.charCodeAt(n) - 48
    var up = false
    if (nxt > 5) { up = true }
    if (nxt == 5) {
        var rest = false
        var i = n + 1
        while (i < ds.length) { if (ds.charCodeAt(i) != 48) { rest = true } i = i + 1 }
        if (rest) { up = true } else {
            var last = ds.charCodeAt(n - 1) - 48
            if (last - Math.floor(last / 2) * 2 == 1) { up = true }
        }
    }
    if (up) {
        var out = ""
        var carry = 1
        var k = n - 1
        while (k >= 0) {
            var d = ds.charCodeAt(k) - 48 + carry
            carry = 0
            if (d == 10) { d = 0; carry = 1 }
            out = "" + d + out
            k = k - 1
        }
        if (carry == 1) { out = ("1" + out).substring(0, n); ex = ex + 1 }
        keep = out
    }
    var endz = keep.length
    while (endz > 1 && keep.charAt(endz - 1) == "0") { endz = endz - 1 }
    return keep.substring(0, endz) + "e" + ex
}

// The value of a DIGITS "e" EXP form. At most nine digits, so the integer part is
// exact; the power of ten is applied in chunks of 10^15, the largest one a double
// holds exactly. The relative error stays under ~1e-15, five million times finer
// than a float's half-ulp - and the only consumer is the round-trip TEST below,
// so an error that small cannot pick a wrong length.
function csDecVal(s) {
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    var ds = s.substring(0, ei)
    var k = parseInt(s.substring(ei + 1), 10) - ds.length + 1
    var m = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < ds.length) { m = m * 10 + (ds.charCodeAt(i) - 48); i = i + 1 }
    while (k >= 15) { m = m * 1000000000000000; k = k - 15 }
    while (k <= 0 - 15) { m = m / 1000000000000000; k = k + 15 }
    while (k > 0) { m = m * 10; k = k - 1 }
    while (k < 0) { m = m / 10; k = k + 1 }
    return m
}

// The shortest decimal that round-trips as a binary32. Nine digits always do.
function csF32Sig(a) {
    var full = csDecDigits(a)
    var n = 1
    while (n < 9) {
        var c = csRoundDig(full, n)
        if (csF32Round(csDecVal(c)) === a) { return c }
        n = n + 1
    }
    return csRoundDig(full, 9)
}

function csF32Str(d) {
    if (d !== d) { return "NaN" }
    var zero = 0
    var inf = 1 / zero
    if (d === inf) { return "Infinity" }
    if (d === 0 - inf) { return "-Infinity" }
    if (d === 0) { if (1 / d < 0) { return "-0" } return "0" }
    var neg = d < 0
    var a = neg ? 0 - d : d
    var sig = csF32Sig(a)
    var ei = sig.indexOf("e")
    var ds = sig.substring(0, ei)
    var e10 = parseInt(sig.substring(ei + 1), 10)
    var body = ""
    // The window: plain inside [1e-5, 1e7). The UPPER bound is the float one (a
    // Single's general-format precision is 7 where a Double's is 15, so 1e7f
    // prints "1E+07" and the double 1e7 prints "10000000"); the LOWER bound is
    // csFloStr's own, unchanged, so the two renderers cannot disagree.
    if (a >= 0.00001 && a < 10000000) {
        body = csF32Plain(ds, e10)
    } else {
        var frac = ds.substring(1)
        var es = e10 < 0 ? (0 - e10) : e10
        var etx = "" + es
        if (es < 10) { etx = "0" + etx }
        body = ds.charAt(0) + (frac == "" ? "" : "." + frac) + "E" + (e10 < 0 ? "-" : "+") + etx
    }
    if (neg) { return "-" + body }
    return body
}

// The plain form, from the digits and the exponent of the first one. There is NO
// forced ".0" - C# writes 100f as "100" - which is the one place this differs in
// shape from java-rt.metajs's jvF32Plain.
function csF32Plain(ds, e10) {
    if (e10 >= 0) {
        var ip = ""
        var i = 0
        while (i <= e10) {
            if (i < ds.length) { ip = ip + ds.charAt(i) } else { ip = ip + "0" }
            i = i + 1
        }
        if (ds.length > e10 + 1) { return ip + "." + ds.substring(e10 + 1) }
        return ip
    }
    var lead = "0."
    var k = 0 - e10 - 1
    while (k > 0) { lead = lead + "0"; k = k - 1 }
    return lead + ds
}

// Every rendering of a tag 14 box goes through here, because the floor cannot
// render style 5 (see the section header).
function csFloText(v) {
    if (floStyle(v) == 5) { return csF32Str(floNum(v)) }
    return floStr(v)
}

// js_csflo32 is the only new extern the width needs: a `1.0f` literal, a
// `(float)e` cast and a `float x = e` declaration all lower to it. Its Go twin is
// registered in abnf/jsrtcsharp.go.
function js_csflo32(v) { return flo(csF32Round(rtjNum(v)), 5) }

// ===================================================== rendering ============

// cspBoolStr is System.Boolean.ToString(): Boolean.TrueString / Boolean.FalseString,
// which are the strings "True" and "False" (the LITERALS true / false are unchanged
// - only the string CONVERSION is capitalised).
function cpBoolStr(b) {
    if (b) { return "True" }
    return "False"
}

// cspStr: the full C# string conversion, the twin of csStr in
// csharp-interpreter.abnf and of cspRender in abnf/jsrtcsharp.go.
function cpStr(v) { return cpRender(v, 0) }

function cpRender(v, depth) {
    if (depth > 32) { return "..." }
    // Console.WriteLine(null) writes an empty line (ECMA-334 12.4.7).
    if (v === undefined) { return "" }
    if (v === null) { return "" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return cpBoolStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return String.fromCharCode(v["__char"]) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return csFloText(v) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "" + v }
    if (typeof v == "function") { return "" + v }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return cpArrayName(v) }
    var s = cpObjStr(v, depth)
    if (s !== undefined) { return s }
    return cpPlainObj(v)
}

// The Go twin's fallthrough for an object shape it does not know is rt.toString,
// which is JavaScript's "[object Object]" - a Dictionary and a tuple land here, and
// both halves already agreed there.
function cpPlainObj(o) { return "[object Object]" }

// cpObjStr renders the object shapes the two C# grammars build, and answers
// undefined for one it does not know.
function cpObjStr(o, depth) {
    // A user ToString() override wins over every built-in rendering, exactly as the
    // virtual System.Object.ToString() does in C#.
    // The __class/__super walk is SHARED - rtFindMethod, lib/runtime.metajs. This
    // file called it 300 lines below and STILL carried a second private copy of
    // the same eleven lines with the name hardcoded to "ToString"; jpFindMember
    // in java-rt.metajs was the third. Byte-identical after normalising the
    // identifiers: the copies' undefined/null/typeof triple IS rtIsObj, and the
    // nested `m !== undefined` test is redundant in front of `typeof m ==
    // "function"`. Zero knobs.
    var m = rtFindMethod(o["__class"], "ToString")
    if (m !== undefined) { return cpRender(m.apply(undefined, [o]), depth + 1) }
    // The type descriptor itself.
    if (o["__isclass"] !== undefined) {
        var n = o["__name"]
        if (typeof n == "string") { return n }
    }
    // An instance: its type's BARE name (see the header of abnf/jsrtcsharp.go -
    // real C# prints the fully qualified name, which this runtime has no concept of).
    var cls = o["__class"]
    if (cls === undefined) { return undefined }
    if (cls === null) { return undefined }
    if (typeof cls != "object") { return undefined }
    var cn = cls["__name"]
    if (typeof cn == "string") { if (cn.length > 0) { return cn } }
    return undefined
}

// ================================== System.Object.Equals / GetHashCode / GetType ==
//
// The twins of csObjEquals / csHash / csTypeOfVal in csharp-interpreter.abnf; the
// comments there are the specification half. Equals is NOT ==: `==` promotes
// (5 == 5L is true), while Int32.Equals(object) requires an Int32, so
// `5.Equals(5L)` is FALSE. cpElemName is the value's own .NET type name, so
// type-strictness is one comparison.
function csUserMethod(recv, name) {
    if (recv === undefined || recv === null) { return undefined }
    if (typeof recv != "object") { return undefined }
    var cls = recv["__class"]
    if (cls === undefined) { return undefined }
    var mm = rtFindMethod(cls, name)
    if (csIsFn(mm)) { return mm }
    return undefined
}
function csObjEquals(a, b) {
    var an = cpElemName(a)
    if (an != "") {
        if (an !== cpElemName(b)) { return false }
        if (csIsChar(a)) { return a["__char"] === b["__char"] }
        if (floIs(a)) {
            var x = rtjNum(a)
            var y = rtjNum(b)
            // Double.Equals is NOT ==: it answers TRUE for two NaNs (documented on
            // System.Double.Equals), which is the one place the two differ.
            if (x !== x) { return y !== y }
            return x === y
        }
        if (sintIs(a) || sintIs(b)) { return csCmp("==", a, b) }
        return a === b
    }
    if (a === undefined || a === null) { return b === undefined || b === null }
    if (csIsRecVal(a) && csIsRecVal(b)) { return csRecEqVal(a, b) }
    return a === b
}
// A record or a ValueTuple: C# gives both VALUE semantics.
function csIsRecVal(v) {
    if (v === null || v === undefined) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    if (v["__tuple"] !== undefined) { return true }
    var c = v["__class"]
    if (c === undefined || c === null) { return false }
    if (typeof c != "object") { return false }
    return c["__isrecord"] === true
}
function csRecKeys(v) {
    if (v["__tuple"] !== undefined) {
        var ks = []
        var n = v["__tuple"]
        var i = 0
        while (i < n) { ks.push("Item" + (i + 1)); i = i + 1 }
        return ks
    }
    var fn = v["__class"]["__fieldNames"]
    if (fn === undefined || fn === null) { return [] }
    return fn
}
function csRecEqVal(a, b) {
    if (a["__tuple"] !== undefined) {
        if (a["__tuple"] !== b["__tuple"]) { return false }
    } else if (a["__class"] !== b["__class"]) { return false }
    var ks = csRecKeys(a)
    var i = 0
    while (i < ks.length) {
        if (!csObjEquals(a[ks[i]], b[ks[i]])) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}
// GetHashCode. ENGINE BEHAVIOUR, deliberately: .NET does not specify these numbers
// and String.GetHashCode is documented to differ between processes, so no assertion
// can name a value. What IS a contract - and what is asserted - is that two values
// Equals() calls equal hash the same.
function csHash(v) {
    if (v === undefined || v === null) { return 0 }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v ? 1 : 0 }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    if (typeof v == "string") {
        var h = 0
        var i = 0
        while (i < v.length) { h = rtWrap32(csMul32(h, 31) + v.charCodeAt(i)); i = i + 1 }
        return h
    }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return rtWrap32(Math.trunc(rtjNum(v))) }
    if (csIsRecVal(v)) {
        var rh = 0
        var ks = csRecKeys(v)
        var k = 0
        while (k < ks.length) { rh = rtWrap32(csMul32(rh, 31) + csHash(v[ks[k]])); k = k + 1 }
        return rh
    }
    return csIdHash(v)
}
// (a * b) mod 2^32 without losing the low bits to a double's 53 significant bits.
function csMul32(a, b) {
    var ah = (a >>> 16) & 65535
    var al = a & 65535
    var bh = (b >>> 16) & 65535
    var bl = b & 65535
    return (((ah * bl + al * bh) << 16) + al * bl) | 0
}
// A per-run identity number for a reference: the k-th distinct object asked gets
// 0x2b3c4d00+k. Reproducible within a run; no assertion may depend on the digits.
var csIdSeq = 0
function csIdHash(v) {
    var h = v["__idhash"]
    if (h !== undefined) { return h }
    csIdSeq = csIdSeq + 1
    v["__idhash"] = 725896960 + csIdSeq
    return v["__idhash"]
}
// GetType(): a System.Type token with the same two members typeof(T) answers.
function csTypeOfVal(v) {
    var full = cpElemName(v)
    if (full == "") {
        if (Array.isArray(v)) { full = cpArrayName(v) }
        else if (v !== undefined && v !== null && typeof v == "object") {
            var c = v["__class"]
            if (c !== undefined && c !== null && typeof c["__name"] == "string") { full = c["__name"] }
            else if (v["__isclass"] === true && typeof v["__name"] == "string") { full = v["__name"] }
            else { full = "System.Object" }
        } else { full = "System.Object" }
    }
    var o = {}
    o["FullName"] = full
    o["Name"] = csLastSeg(full)
    return o
}
function csLastSeg(s) {
    var i = s.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        if (s.charAt(i) == ".") { return s.substring(i + 1) }
        i = i - 1
    }
    return s
}

// cspArrayName is Object.ToString() of an array: the element type's full name plus
// "[]". The DECLARED element type is not carried by this value model, so it is
// INFERRED from the elements - see the header of abnf/jsrtcsharp.go for the rule
// and for what real C# would answer instead.
function cpArrayName(a) {
    if (a.length == 0) { return "System.Object[]" }
    var name = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var n = cpElemName(a[i])
        if (n == "") { return "System.Object[]" }
        if (name != "") { if (n != name) { return "System.Object[]" } }
        name = n
        i = i + 1
    }
    return name + "[]"
}

function cpElemName(e) {
    if (csIsChar(e)) { return "System.Char" }
    if (typeof e == "boolean") { return "System.Boolean" }
    if (typeof e == "string") { return "System.String" }
    // ECMA-334 8.3.7: a `float` is System.Single. A `decimal` is still a double
    // box in this runtime, so decimal[] answers System.Double[] - unchanged.
    if (floIs(e)) { if (floStyle(e) == 5) { return "System.Single" } return "System.Double" }
    if (sintIs(e)) {
        // A sized integer carries its own type, so long[] / uint[] / byte[] are the
        // one array kind whose element type is not a guess.
        var w = sintWidth(e)
        var u = sintUns(e)
        if (w == 64) { return u ? "System.UInt64" : "System.Int64" }
        if (w == 32) { return u ? "System.UInt32" : "System.Int32" }
        if (w == 16) { return u ? "System.UInt16" : "System.Int16" }
        return u ? "System.Byte" : "System.SByte"
    }
    if (typeof e == "number") { return "System.Int32" }
    return ""
}

// csString (abnf/jsrt.go): the rendering js_csadd / js_csadd2 apply to an operand of
// '+'. A null operand contributes the EMPTY STRING (ECMA-334 12.4.7), everything
// else goes through the shared toString - which for the values js_csstr has already
// let through is the number, the sized integer, the boxed double or the char.
function cpAddStr(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return "" }
    if (v === null) { return "" }
    return cpToString(v)
}

// The shared runtime's own toString, which is what rt.toString answers for each of
// the shapes that can still reach it here.
function cpToString(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v ? "true" : "false" }
    if (csIsChar(v)) { return String.fromCharCode(v["__char"]) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return csFloText(v) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "" + v }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return cpJoin(v) }
    return "" + v
}

// rt.toString of an array is its elements joined with a comma. There is no join in
// this dialect, so it is written out.
function cpJoin(a) {
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        if (i > 0) { out = out + "," }
        out = out + cpToString(a[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ===================================================== the integral types ===

// csTypeW / csTypeU: C#'s `byte` is UNSIGNED (0..255) and `sbyte` is the signed one
// - the opposite of Java, and the single most common way to get this wrong. The
// names are prefixed so that no property of Object.prototype can be hit by mistake.
function csTypeW(name) {
    if (name == "sbyte") { return 8 }
    if (name == "byte") { return 8 }
    if (name == "short") { return 16 }
    if (name == "ushort") { return 16 }
    if (name == "int") { return 32 }
    if (name == "uint") { return 32 }
    if (name == "long") { return 64 }
    if (name == "ulong") { return 64 }
    if (name == "nint") { return 64 }
    if (name == "nuint") { return 64 }
    return 0
}

function csTypeU(name) {
    if (name == "byte") { return true }
    if (name == "ushort") { return true }
    if (name == "uint") { return true }
    if (name == "ulong") { return true }
    if (name == "nuint") { return true }
    return false
}

function csTypeAlias(name) {
    if (name == "SByte") { return "sbyte" }
    if (name == "Byte") { return "byte" }
    if (name == "Int16") { return "short" }
    if (name == "UInt16") { return "ushort" }
    if (name == "Int32") { return "int" }
    if (name == "UInt32") { return "uint" }
    if (name == "Int64") { return "long" }
    if (name == "UInt64") { return "ulong" }
    return name
}

// csParseInt is int.Parse / long.Parse / uint.Parse: a decimal digit run with an
// optional sign, and a System.FormatException on anything else.
function csParseInt(s, w, u) {
    var t = s.trim()
    var neg = false
    var i = 0
    if (t.length > 0) {
        var c0 = t.charAt(0)
        if (c0 == "+") { i = 1 } else if (c0 == "-") {
            neg = true
            i = 1
        }
    }
    if (i >= t.length) {
        csThrow("FormatException", "The input string was not in a correct format.")
    }
    var acc = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, true)
    var ten = sintRaw(0, 10, 64, true)
    while (i < t.length) {
        var d = t.charCodeAt(i) - 48
        if (d < 0 || d > 9) {
            csThrow("FormatException", "The input string was not in a correct format.")
        }
        acc = sintOp(0, sintOp(2, acc, ten), sintRaw(0, d, 64, true))
        i = i + 1
    }
    // The Go twin accumulates in a uint64 and then reads the result as int64, so the
    // negation is the two's complement one at 64 bits.
    if (neg) { acc = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, true), acc) }
    return csNorm(acc, w, u)
}

// csTypeObject: one of the integral type NAMES as an expression primary, so that
// `int.MaxValue`, `ulong.MaxValue` and `int.Parse(s)` resolve.
function csTypeObject(name0) {
    var o = {}
    if (name0 == "char" || name0 == "Char") {
        o["MinValue"] = csChar(0)
        o["MaxValue"] = csChar(65535)
        return o
    }
    var name = csTypeAlias(name0)
    var w = csTypeW(name)
    if (w == 0) { return o }
    var un = csTypeU(name)
    var lo = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false)
    var hi = sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false)
    if (un) {
        // int64(-1) at width 64, i.e. every bit set; the narrower widths mask it.
        hi = csAllOnes()
    } else {
        // 2^(w-1) - 1 and its negation, built from the all-ones pattern so that no
        // exponent literal is needed.
        hi = sintOp(10, sintRaw(2147483647, 4294967295, 64, false), 64 - w)
        lo = sintOp(1, sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false), sintOp(0, hi, sintRaw(0, 1, 64, false)))
    }
    o["MinValue"] = csNorm(lo, w, un)
    o["MaxValue"] = csNorm(hi, w, un)
    o["Parse"] = function(s) { return csParseInt(cpStr(s), w, un) }
    return o
}

// ===================================================== the exports ==========
//
// Everything below is a TOP-LEVEL `function js_*`, which is what -rt-lib exports
// under its own C symbol. The order follows abnf/jsrtcsharp.go and the shared block
// of abnf/jsrt.go.

// ----- the language-NEUTRAL fifteen -----------------------------------------
// None of these is C#'s. They live in abnf/jsrt.go, several languages emit them,
// and they are here only because the C floor does not have them - see the extern
// split in docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3.

// js_arr_new_n is SHARED with java - lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. Once csNum and jvNum
// turned out to be the same function, the two copies had zero differences.

// js_char and js_char_code are SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs. js_char was the same
// one-line delegation in three languages; js_char_code's leading csIsChar test was
// dead, because rtjNum's second arm already unboxes a char.

// An empty Dictionary<K,V> / Python dict / Go map handle.
// js_dict_new is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs (six identical copies, no knobs).

function csIsDict(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return false }
    return v["__dict"] !== undefined
}

// The dict's key lookup, by VALUE - two boxes holding 5 are one key.
function csDictFind(keys, k) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < keys.length) {
        if (csValueEq(keys[i], k)) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// rt.dictFind's equality: strict for everything the floor knows, by value for the
// two number tags and the char box.
function csValueEq(a, b) {
    if (csIsChar(a) || csIsChar(b)) { return js_jchareq(a, b) }
    if (floIs(a) || floIs(b)) { return floEq(a, b) }
    if (sintIs(a) || sintIs(b)) {
        if (!rtjIsIntegral(a)) { return false }
        if (!rtjIsIntegral(b)) { return false }
        return csCmp("==", a, b)
    }
    return a === b
}

// Sequence indexing and dict lookup. C#'s emitter routes every subscript through
// this (core.indexExt).
function js_pyget(c, k) {
    if (csIsDict(c)) {
        var i = csDictFind(c["keys"], k)
        if (i < 0) { fail("KeyError: " + cpStr(k)) }
        return c["vals"][i]
    }
    var idx = rtjNum(k)
    if (Array.isArray(c)) {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list index out of range") }
        return c[idx]
    }
    if (typeof c == "string") {
        if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
        if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("string index out of range") }
        return c.charAt(idx)
    }
    fail("indexing a " + typeof c)
    return undefined
}

// js_pyset is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs (five languages, knobs rtkIsDict and
// rtkDictFind; the index coercion now truncates, which is the oracle).

function js_pylen(v) {
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v.length }
    if (csIsDict(v)) { return v["keys"].length }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return v.length }
    fail("len() of a " + typeof v)
    return 0
}

// 'key in obj' / hasOwnProperty: own properties of an object, index and 'length' of
// an array or string. csharp-to-llvm-ir emits it for exactly one probe, "does this
// receiver carry a __class", so the object arm is the only one it reaches.
function js_has(o, key) {
    var k = cpToString(key)
    if (typeof o == "string") {
        if (k == "length") { return true }
        var si = rtjNum(key)
        if (si !== si) { return false }
        return si == Math.trunc(si) && si >= 0 && si < o.length
    }
    if (Array.isArray(o)) {
        if (k == "length") { return true }
        var ai = rtjNum(key)
        if (ai !== ai) { return false }
        return ai == Math.trunc(ai) && ai >= 0 && ai < o.length
    }
    if (o === undefined || o === null) { return false }
    if (typeof o != "object") { return false }
    return o[k] !== undefined
}

// THIS USED TO WALK THE __class CHAIN FOR A '__get_<k>' / '__set_<k>' MEMBER, and
// that was a silent halves divergence: abnf/jsrt.go's js_has walks the chain too,
// but through findClassAccessor, which answers only for a *jsAccessor - the
// defineProperty accessor of the JS value model. A C# property with accessor bodies
// is NOT one of those; it is a plain '__get_X' CLOSURE on the descriptor, so the Go
// twin answered false where this answered true. Nothing reached it while the only
// probe csharp-to-llvm-ir emitted was `js_has(x, "__class")`; the property/field
// name-collision guard (docs/todo.md 1.10) asks about a member name, and needs the
// twin's answer - "does the receiver carry an own slot of this name", which is
// exactly what tells a plain field apart from a property with accessor bodies.

// The dynamic type test behind `is` / a type pattern. abnf/jsrt.go's isTypeName is
// the spec, including the part that surprises: the Int/Long/Short/Byte arm tests for
// a PLAIN number, so a boxed long is NOT `is Int` there either.
// js_is_type is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkIsChar/rtkIsArr hooks.

// The BCL name of each integral type keyword, so `o is Int64` answers what
// `o is long` answers (ECMA-334 8.3.5: the keywords are aliases).
function csIsTyAlias(t) {
    if (t == "SByte") { return "sbyte" }
    if (t == "Byte") { return "byte" }
    if (t == "Int16") { return "short" }
    if (t == "UInt16") { return "ushort" }
    if (t == "Int32") { return "int" }
    if (t == "UInt32") { return "uint" }
    if (t == "Int64") { return "long" }
    if (t == "UInt64") { return "ulong" }
    return t
}
// js_csis(v, t) is `v is T` / `v as T` / a type pattern. ECMA-334 12.12.12 asks for
// the RUN-TIME type of v - there is no implicit conversion in a type test, which is
// why `object o = 5; o is long` is false in C# - and the shared js_is_type cannot
// answer that for a number: it says "is it an integral number" for all eight
// integral names at once and "is it a number" for all three floating ones, so
// `1.5f is float`, `1.5 is double`, `5L is long` and `(byte)3 is byte` were all
// FALSE while `5 is long` was true (docs/todo.md 1.9).
//
// This value model does carry the answer: a plain number is an `int`, a sized
// integer is every other integral type and carries its width and signedness, and a
// float box's style byte says double (2) or float (5). `is decimal` is answered by
// the `double` arm, because a decimal IS a style-2 double box here - the one residue
// this cannot fix without a decimal representation of its own. Every non-numeric
// name falls through to the shared probe unchanged. The twins are js_csis in
// abnf/jsrtcsharp.go and csIsType in languages/csharp-interpreter.abnf.
function js_csis(v, tname) {
    var t = csIsTyAlias(csStripAngleTy(tname))
    var opt = false
    if (t.length > 0 && t.charAt(t.length - 1) == "?") { t = t.substring(0, t.length - 1); opt = true }
    var isF32 = t == "float" || t == "Single"
    var isFlo = t == "double" || t == "Double" || t == "decimal" || t == "Decimal"
    // `char` is answered here too: the shared probe's "Char" arm answers for a PLAIN
    // NUMBER (the Java/Go reading, where a char is one), so `1 is char` came back
    // TRUE in this half and FALSE in the interpreter, which has a char box. A
    // pre-existing halves divergence --cross never reached because no test asks.
    var isChr = t == "char" || t == "Char"
    var bits = csTypeW(t)
    if (bits == 0 && !isFlo && !isF32 && !isChr) { return js_is_type(v, tname) }
    if (v === null) { return opt }
    if (v === undefined) { return opt }
    if (isChr) { return rtIsCharBox(v) }
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    if (floIs(v)) {
        if (floStyle(v) == 5) { return isF32 }
        return isFlo
    }
    if (isFlo || isF32) { return false }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintWidth(v) == bits && sintUns(v) === csTypeU(t) }
